    bodyEn: `Almost every machine sold in the last decade reports home: CAT VisionLink, Komatsu KOMTRAX, Volvo CareTrack, Doosan/Develon telematics, Hitachi ConSite. In most Saudi fleets that data flows into an inbox nobody reads. Yet three of its streams convert directly into parts money — no data science required.

## Stream One: Hours, Automatically Honest

Parts planning runs on machine hours, and manually collected hour readings are chronically late and wrong. Telematics fixes that silently. What it enables:

- **Service kits ordered by projection, not surprise.** If the PC300 is at 7,830 hours and averages 210/month, the 8,000-hour kit should be ordered now — the calendar writes itself for the whole fleet
- **Wear-part budgeting per real utilization.** Undercarriage, GET, and tire budgets keyed to actual hours instead of assumed ones stop both overstocking and shortage
- **Warranty and rebuild timing** decided on facts

## Stream Two: Fault Codes, Triaged

Modern machines log fault codes long before failure becomes visible. The discipline that works is simple triage: ignore the noise (momentary low-voltage events at cranking), watch the trends (repeated intermittent codes on one sensor family), and act on the escalators — DPF pressure trends, fuel rail deviations, hydraulic temperature excursions. Each escalator maps to a parts decision made early: an injector set air-freighted this week beats a towed machine next month.

## Stream Three: Fuel and Idle Data

Fuel burn per hour is a free health monitor: a machine burning 8% above its baseline is telling you about air restriction, injector wear, or dragging brakes. Idle ratio is a parts predictor too — high-idle fleets carbon up aftertreatment systems and glaze cylinders, and their DPF and EGR parts demand shows it. The data flags which machines, and operator coaching plus timely filters change the curve.

## A Starter Workflow That Fits in One Hour a Week

1. Export hours for all units; update the service projection sheet
2. Review new fault codes against the triage rules; flag escalators
3. Compare fuel burn to each machine's baseline; investigate outliers
4. Convert flags into one consolidated parts order and next month's service plan

That single hour, done weekly, is the highest-return maintenance activity a Saudi fleet office can run.

HESP works with telematics-literate fleets — send us your projected service calendar and we will hold kits against it, or share the fault-code flags and we will quote the fix parts proactively. Machine data plus parts supply, working as one system: that is the point.`,
